        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) could serve as the primary tool for non-invasively quantifying and monitoring anatomic and functional changes associated with low gravity in a controlled space environment in longitudinal studies, and permit the quantitative assessment of interventions designed to counteract such changes in animal models. Thus, the goal of this project is to build a space-qualifiable prototype MRI system with an imaging aperture of 5-6 cm. Target weight and power requirements for the initial version are <150 kg, and < 1.5 kW respectively. We plan to base the system on a commercial MRI spectrometer, which will greatly reduce costs associated with sophisticated MRI pulse sequence programming, and the development of high performance RF and interface hard-ware. This will shift the focus of the effort to efficient power and weight design. Magnet manufacturers are working on HTS magnets, and we believe that this solution will be available in the future to provide a light-weight magnet. Since many of the changes caused by low gravity involve anatomical and functional changes affecting internal organs, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) would provide a primary yard-stick for non-invasively quantifying anatomic and physiological parameters in a real, controlled, space environment in longitudinal studies, and permit the quantitative assessment of interventions designed to counteract these changes in animal models. Thus, the goal of this project is to build a space qualifiable proto-type MRI system with an imaging aperture suitable for imaging rodents in animal models. 
